The Beat Before the Bells

Every sixty beats, the three towers rang together, and the sea stood up to listen.

It rose on either side of the causeway in two green walls. By daylight you could see fish turning in the water above your head. At night, when the moon was low, you could walk across the harbor with a constellation on either side of you and hear the shrimp clicking among the stars.

Iona had crossed that way with her mother since she was small enough to be carried.

Her mother used to stop at the middle and say, “Listen. You can hear how tall the water is.”

Iona could. The little sharp sounds came from near her ankles. The great slow ones came from far overhead.

Tonight something up there was singing.

And something down on the exposed harbor floor was trying to sing back.

