In the first tower, a boy struck a bronze bell every third beat. One beat before each strike, he drew his thumbnail across the rim. His teacher had taught him to do it so he’d know the bell was steady enough to hit.

In the second tower, a woman hauled back an oak clapper every fourth beat. One beat before it fell, the rope creaked. Her late husband had offered to oil that pulley every winter for forty years. She had always told him she liked to hear him coming.

In the third tower, Iona’s mother held the lowest note in the harbor. Her bell sounded every fifth beat. One beat before it sounded, she drew a breath so deep that the shutters lifted.

On the sixtieth beat, bronze, oak, and breath met. The bells held the sea in place for another minute.

Below, stranded between the walls of its own ocean, a whale calf opened its mouth.

Its mother sang again. Iona put a hand on the causeway railing and felt the note in her teeth.

