Over the three bells hung a single red cord. A pull would lift all three hammers clear. After the first hammer fell, the cord locked until the last had finished ringing. There was one safe instant to pull all three away from the final strike. The one when they were all drawn back.

Iona found the cord where it entered the conductor’s box. Three strands, braided. The bronze tower’s strand was smooth. The oak tower’s had a knot in it. Her mother had stitched a row of tiny beads into hers so Iona could find it with cold fingers.

“Wait for the chord,” the conductor said.

Iona had heard that instruction at every rehearsal of her life. The chord was how you knew everyone had arrived.

She put her fingers on the three strands.

The smooth one twitched.

Then the knotted one.

Then the beads.

All evening she’d been listening to the bells. Now she listened to what each player did one beat before.

The boy scratched the rim.

The woman drew back the weight.

Her mother took a breath.

Iona pictured the three of them doing it together. She could hear the sound it would make. A little scrape. A creak. Her mother drawing in the entire night.

One beat before the chord.

She ran out onto the gallery. “Clear the causeway!”

The conductor caught up with her at the rail. For a moment he looked down at all the people still crossing. Then he took the speaking trumpet from its hook.

“You heard her,” he said.

People scrambled up the steps at either end. Two dockhands carried an old man between them. A girl went back for a shoe and came up barefoot, carrying both shoes in her hands. At the far end of the causeway she turned and waved them over her head.

Iona put her hand back on the cord.

The whale sang. The calf’s answer was so faint she felt it through the rail before she heard it.

