A scrape.

A creak.

A breath.

Iona pulled.

The sixtieth beat came, and the whole harbor heard what it sounded like when three bells didn’t ring.

For an instant the sea stood exactly where it had been.

Then both walls leaned inward. Their high edges curled together, and the moon broke into a thousand white pieces between them. The water met above the causeway. A ribbon of bubbles ran the length of the streetlights and went dark one lamp at a time.

Iona felt something rise through the railing. She thought it was the calf calling again.

Then she felt a second note beneath the first, almost close enough to be the same note. They held there together. Farther out, beyond the towers, two backs broke the surface. The larger one rolled, and for an instant the moon shone along the length of an eye.

At the top of the steps the barefoot girl was crying. She had put one shoe on each hand. She clapped them together, just once.

Up in the third tower, Iona’s mother laughed hard enough to lose the breath she’d been holding.

